@@ -75,6 +75,8 @@ It plays back through the project's own replay path: `node tools/preview/blackbo
 
 **The checkpoint hashes leave the frame (2026-11-14).** A saved match's replay checkpoints are SimHash's gameplay hash every 60 ticks. SimHash walks every column of the planet (about 25 ms at medium, four times the columns at huge), and the session used to run it in the frame, on every run of a checkpoint tick, so a LAN match could hitch once a second and again in a rollback that re-ran one. Now the session hashes each checkpoint once, when it becomes final, from the copy the checkpoint already holds, on a worker thread (`NetSession._hashLater`); a saved file waits for any job still running (`hashesDone`). The values and their order are the same: `lan_check` plays the same match both ways and finds both PCs' replays byte for byte the same, with none of the hashes on the game's thread, and a control that hashes the present state instead is caught.
 
+**Where the HUD says reports go (UI's S-04, 2026-11-20).** On a desktop build UI's HUD names the folder the recorder writes (`user://reports`). The LAN kit sets `ui_hud.report_lan = true` with its report hook, so a LAN game says "Saved reports go in the 'saved' folder beside the game.", and it writes the HUD's sentence into the LAN log at the match's start (`report where: ...`). The kit's rows check that line. `--lan-no-report-lan` (tests only) leaves the flag off: the control, which shows the desktop folder.
+
 **The build's stamp (A-NET-05, 2026-11-14).** Each LAN folder's game names its build once, in `res://build_info.json`, the file `SimReplay.buildId()` reads. The kit writes it in the shape of Tools' page stamp: `{commit (7), date (the commit's), stack, lan: true, built (the kit's own UTC time), id}`. The id is the page's own (`<commit8>-<cache key 8>`): the kit changes only render and UI files, so the sim, data and net a replay runs on are the page's, and a LAN save plays on the page (Tools' LAN report clips) and on its kept tree too (the stand-in's B1 review, finding 1). `built` tells one LAN folder from another. Both PCs, running the same folder, share the stamp. A saved file's `build` is that stamp and its replay's `build` is the id; the file name keeps the commit's first 8 from the id. The kit writes the same stamp into the folder's `tree/` (the game's sim, data, net, project and class cache as exported, read-only), so a saved match plays back there. Anywhere else (another build, a dev tree) it is refused as another build's before its first tick, with both ids, the same reason a solo replay gets; UI writes the sentence. `--build` is needed only for a tree with no page stamp. `lan_check` loads a foreign LAN save from disk and checks the refusal, with a control that leaves the build check out.
 
